  {
    "repo": "stm32f4xx-hal-driver",
    "pr_number": 43,
    "title": "Add beginner-friendly GPIO LED blink example with register explanations",
    "state": "closed",
    "merged": false,
    "draft": false,
    "author": "MrAbhi19",
    "labels": [
      "enhancement",
      "invalid",
      "hal",
      "gpio"
    ],
    "created_at": "2025-11-26T03:13:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-11-28T10:12:52Z",
    "closed_at": "2025-11-28T10:12:31Z",
    "merged_at": null,
    "body": "This PR introduces a simple demo in /Src/examples/stm32f4xx_hal_led.c that toggles an LED using STM32F4 HAL functions. The example is designed for beginners and includes clear comments explaining the underlying register-level operations (RCC_AHB1ENR, GPIOx_MODER, GPIOx_ODR, etc.).\r\n\r\nDetails:\r\n\r\n- Added new source file: /Src/examples/stm32f4xx_hal_led.c\r\n- Updated README.md with a section highlighting the example\r\n- Example assumes LED on GPIOD Pin 12 (STM32F4 Discovery board), but can be adapted to other pins/boards\r\n- Comments connect HAL abstractions with hardware registers for educational clarity\r\n\r\nMotivation: \r\nThe goal is to make the repository more accessible to students and first-time contributors by providing a reproducible, beginner-friendly demo. This contribution aligns with the repo’s mission of offering HAL drivers and examples that bridge abstraction with hardware logic.\r\n\r\nImpact:\r\n- Helps newcomers understand how HAL functions map to registers\r\n- Provides a ready-to-run LED blink demo for testing setup\r\n- Strengthens educational value of the STM32F4 HAL driver repo",
